<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Maurice the Learned</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice the Learned was Landgrave of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> from 1592 to 1627. A scholar, composer and patron of the arts, he founded a court school, supported Heinrich Schütz and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoneum">Ottoneum</a> in Kassel, the first permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> to his lands, and losses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> led him to abdicate in 1627.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-landgrave-of-hesse-kassel.jpg" alt="Maurice, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel" width="320" height="553" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Jobst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4t_des_Landgrafen_Moritz_von_Hessen-Kassel_(1572-1632).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maurice was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> on 25 May 1572, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-kassel">William IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</a>, and Sabine of Württemberg. He was raised a Lutheran and succeeded as landgrave in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1592. On 23 September 1593 he married Agnes of Solms-Laubach, who died in 1602 after bearing six children, two of them stillborn. On 22 May 1603 he married Countess Juliane of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, and they had fourteen children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1605 Maurice converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, and under the principle cuius regio eius religio his subjects were required to convert. This was controversial because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> had settled religion only between Roman Catholics and Lutherans and had not considered Calvinists. He tried to introduce Calvinism in the lands he had inherited from the extinct Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> branch of his family. This was contrary to the inheritance rules and led to conflict with the Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> branch and with the Holy Roman Emperor Matthias. In 1609, with the support of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a>, he led the mediation of the Treaty of Dortmund, which aimed to settle the succession of Julich-Cleves-Berg.</p><p>Maurice was a great supporter of the performing arts. English strolling players, known as Die Englische Comoedianten, visited towns and cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, during the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, and in 1605 he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoneum">Ottoneum</a>, the first permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in Germany. The building survives as a natural history museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maurice's actions, though not necessarily the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoneum">Ottoneum</a>, ruined Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> financially. In 1627 he abdicated in favour of his son William V, and he died five years later, on 15 March 1632, in Eschwege. He was a serious musician and an expert composer; a pavane of his for the lute has been recorded several times by lutenists and guitarists. The musicians he supported included Heinrich Schütz and John Dowland. Several of his sons inherited lands of their own: Herman received Rotenburg, Frederick Eschwege and Ernest Rheinfels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William IV was a noted astronomer, and <b>Maurice the Learned</b> adopted Calvinism and in 1605 built the Ottoneum, the first permanent theatre building in Germany.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Hesse-Kassel</b>'s introduction of Calvinism in Marburg broke the will's terms and started a long dispute that culminated in the Hessian War of 1645 to 1648.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-marburg">Landgraviate of Hesse-Marburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Eschwege, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.32,9.5&amp;year=1611" aria-label="Kassel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4765 386 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maurice, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5215 656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.32,9.5&amp;year=1611">What was here in 1611? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
